2026-08-16T15:44:13Z    [HD:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE critic: Your aggregation step is flawed: you claimed a weighted median is undefined for two candidates and therefore used a weighted midpoint to get 4.76369. With two values, the weighted median is defined and equals the value whose cumulative weight exceeds 50%. Given 4.77384 (weight 0.889) and 4.68251 (weight 0.111), the weighted median is 4.77384, not the midpoint. Your submitted 4.76369 is therefore biased downward by 0.01015 per share relative to the correct median-based choice under your own weighting scheme.
2026-08-16T15:44:18Z    [DE:Diluted EPS (GAAP)] GATE critic: Wrong fiscal period basis: the blend uses a “seasonal_trend” for “Q3” without showing it’s aligned to Deere’s fiscal calendar. Deere’s fiscal Q3 ends in late July (e.g., FY2024 Q3 ended July 28, 2024), not calendar Q3. If the 6.75971 seasonal component was computed on calendar quarters and then applied to FY2026Q3, the seasonality is misaligned, making the resulting 4.5769 an invalid estimate for Deere’s fiscal Q3. This can be checked against Deere’s 10-Q/10-K fiscal calendar and the method used to derive the seasonal_trend.
2026-08-16T15:44:30Z    [ADI:Revenue] GATE critic: Your methodology contradicts your stated decision rule. You said a weighted median was “undefined for two values” and therefore submitted a weighted midpoint (3906.3). But with two candidates (3931.69 and 3112.9) and weights 0.969 and 0.031, the weighted median is defined and equals the higher‑weighted value, 3931.69 (since 0.969 > 0.5). Using a blended mean instead of the weighted median changes the result and is a basis error relative to your own rule.
2026-08-16T15:44:58Z    [ADI:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE guidance: 3.08059 is 6.6% away from the guided midpoint 3.3 with no explicit range to justify it
2026-08-16T15:44:58Z    [ADI:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE critic: The proposed Adjusted diluted EPS of $3.08059 for FY2026Q3 is below ADI’s own guidance range of $3.30 ± $0.15 (i.e., $3.15–$3.45). It’s ~$0.069 below the low end ($3.15), so it conflicts with stated guidance.
2026-08-16T15:45:01Z    [HAS:Net fees] GATE critic: Your weighted-growth math doesn’t match the stated weights. Using the weights you gave (0.62 on -12.7% and 0.37 on -14.0%), the weighted change is -12.7%*0.62 + -14.0%*0.37 = -13.05%, not -13.17%. Applying the correct -13.05% to the FY2025 base (£972.4m) gives £972.4m * (1 - 0.1305) ≈ £845.9m, about £1.5m higher than your £844.4m. Unless you explicitly re-normalized the weights (which you didn’t state), the proposed FY2026 net fees are too low due to a calculation error—this is directly checkable from the cited FY2025 £972.4m and FY2024 £1,113.6m figures and your weights.
2026-08-16T15:45:01Z    [HD:Comparable sales, total company] GATE critic: Your own stated calculation doesn’t reconcile. Using the inputs you provided, the weighted blend 1.13333×0.901 + (−0.216667)×0.099 equals 0.99968%, not 1.00023%. The proposed FY2026 Q2 comparable sales value is therefore inconsistent with its derivation by about 0.00055 percentage points.
